---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const DISCORD = 'https://discord.gg/etmb7DYbj';
const DISCUSS = 'https://github.com/Chronophage-net/fates-edge-dev/discussions';
---
<BaseLayout title="Welcome">
  <div class="cta-hero">
    <div class="eyebrow">The workshop door is open</div>
    <h1>Come in and put your coat somewhere.</h1>
    <p class="tagline">
      This is where Fate&rsquo;s Edge actually gets made &mdash; the rules engine, the clients, the AI GM
      that you never have to turn on, and the arguments about all three. It is open, it is unfinished
      on purpose, and there is more of it than one person can hold.
    </p>
    <p class="principle">Every layer should be replaceable, hackable, and worth someone else&rsquo;s time.</p>
    <div class="btn-row">
      <a class="btn btn-gold" href="#ways-in">Find your way in</a>
      <a class="btn btn-outline" href="/architecture">Read the architecture</a>
      <a class="btn btn-outline" href={DISCUSS}>Say hello</a>
      <a class="btn btn-outline" href={DISCORD}>Discord</a>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Before anything else</strong>
      <p style="margin:0.6rem 0 0;">
        I am one person. I read every issue and every pull request, usually within a day or two, and I
        have never once been annoyed that someone opened one. If you are sitting on a question because it
        seems too small, it is not too small. Ask it in <a href={DISCUSS}>Discussions</a> or on
        <a href={DISCORD}>Discord</a> and I will answer.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="ways-in">
    <h2>Three ways in</h2>
    <p class="lead">Most projects like this only have a door for developers. This one has three, and none of them is the service entrance.</p>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="door">
        <h3>You want to build things</h3>
        <p>
          A deterministic rules engine with no hidden GM logic, a transport layer that already speaks
          web, terminal, Discord, Roll20, Foundry and Python, a tag-based GM protocol, and a modular AI
          GM that is driver-agnostic and entirely optional. Every subsystem is small enough to read in
          an afternoon.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/architecture">Architecture</a> &middot; <a href="/contribute">Ways to contribute</a> &middot; <a href="/workflow">How work moves</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="door">
        <h3>You want to play it and tell me what broke</h3>
        <p>
          Playtesters are not a QA tier here. Most of what has changed in the last year changed because
          somebody ran a session and said the middle of it dragged, or that a rule read one way at the
          table and another way on the page. You do not need to file a good bug report. &ldquo;This bit
          was confusing and here is roughly where&rdquo; is genuinely enough.
        </p>
        <p><a href={DISCORD}>Bring it to Discord</a> &middot; <a href={DISCUSS}>or open a discussion</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="door">
        <h3>You design, write, or draw</h3>
        <p>
          Adventure modules are JSON, rules-native, and AI-optional &mdash; you can write one without
          touching a line of engine code. The setting has an art bible and a community content policy
          that is meant to let you publish your own work, not to fence it off.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/art-bible">Art bible</a> &middot; <a href="/call-for-art">Call for art</a> &middot; <a href="/community-content">Community content</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>The one thing I genuinely cannot do myself</strong>
      <p style="margin:0.6rem 0 0;">
        Architecture I can manage. Moderation, onboarding, and keeping a growing
        <a href={DISCORD}>Discord</a> safe and welcoming is not my skill set, and at the moment it is me
        guessing. If that is the thing you are actually good at, it is the most useful job on this
        project and nobody is doing it. See the <a href="/contribute#community-care">full ask</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2>What you can expect from me</h2>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="feature-card"><h3>Clear boundaries</h3><p>A layered architecture rather than a tangle. You can change one thing without reading everything.</p></div>
      <div class="feature-card"><h3>A reply</h3><p>Every issue and PR gets read by a human, which at present is the only kind of person here.</p></div>
      <div class="feature-card"><h3>A safety net</h3><p>A test-covered core, so a first contribution is unlikely to break something quietly.</p></div>
      <div class="feature-card"><h3>Credit</h3><p>Your name on what you made, and a licence that lets you keep making it elsewhere.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Starting from nothing</strong>
      <ol style="margin:0.75rem 0 0 1.2rem; color:var(--text);">
        <li>Clone whichever repo below matches the door you came in by.</li>
        <li>Read the <a href="/architecture">architecture overview</a> &mdash; it is short.</li>
        <li>Pick the subsystem that looked interesting and ignore the rest for now.</li>
        <li>Open an issue or a draft PR. Small ones are welcome and are how most people start.</li>
        <li>Check the <a href="/workflow">workflow</a> for how Discuss &rarr; Issue &rarr; PR maps onto the repos.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <ul class="repo-list">
      <li><a href="https://github.com/Chronophage-net/fates-edge-ai-gm-bot">fates-edge-ai-gm-bot</a> &mdash; rules engine, AI GM, transport layer</li>
      <li><a href="https://github.com/Chronophage-net/fates-edge-apps">fates-edge-apps</a> &mdash; web client, terminal client, tools</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="text-align:center; padding-block:1rem 3rem;">
    <p class="lead" style="margin:0 auto;">
      The game is free, in every sense that matters and most of the ones that do not. If you build
      something on top of it, I would like to see it.
    </p>
    <div class="btn-row" style="justify-content:center; margin-top:1rem;">
      <a class="btn btn-gold" href={DISCUSS}>Say hello in Discussions</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
